# Citation metadata for SNES-MDB.
#
# `doi` is deliberately ABSENT. A DOI requires a hosting decision, which is
# gated on the unresolved rights review for the musical corpus (see
# RIGHTS.md). It will be added when one is assigned, not before.
#
# `version` describes THE SOFTWARE, because `type` is software. An earlier
# draft put `snesmdb-50game-rc1` here, which is a frozen *corpus* release
# label -- a reader would have cited the dataset while the entry described
# the code, and the two version in different units. The corpus release this
# code produced is named in the abstract instead, where it cannot be
# mistaken for the software's own version.
#
# 0.1.0 is the first release-ready state of the toolkit. The repository
# carries no tags, so this is the project's declared starting version rather
# than a reading of its history.
#
# Nothing here asserts that the corpus may be redistributed.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use SNES-MDB, please cite it using the metadata in this file.
title: "SNES-MDB: a symbolic music database for the Super Nintendo Entertainment System"
abstract: >-
  A symbolic music corpus extracted from SNES/Super Famicom SPC files,
  intended for musical analysis and machine learning without requiring
  knowledge of SPC700 or S-DSP internals. Sample identity is resolved from
  the directory entry the S-DSP reads at key-on, so an identity is a
  property of the attack rather than of a hardware index. Uncertainty is
  published explicitly: an unestablished identity is null with a reason, and
  an unrecovered pitch is marked rather than estimated. This version of the
  software produced the corpus release `snesmdb-50game-rc1`: 2,228 tracks
  from 50 games, 3,499,685 events.
type: software
license: MIT
license-url: "https://spdx.org/licenses/MIT.html"
repository-code: "https://github.com/uestraven/snesmdb"

authors:
  - family-names: Cohen
    given-names: Paul

version: "0.1.0"
date-released: "2026-11-10"

keywords:
  - symbolic music
  - music information retrieval
  - Super Nintendo
  - SPC700
  - S-DSP
  - music generation
  - video game music

# Prior and third-party work SNES-MDB builds on. These are ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS,
# not substitutes: cite SNES-MDB for SNES-MDB. Cite NES-MDB additionally if
# the design lineage or the split convention matters to your work.
references:
  - type: conference-paper
    title: >-
      The NES Music Database: A multi-instrumental dataset with expressive
      performance attributes
    authors:
      - family-names: Donahue
        given-names: Chris
      - family-names: Mao
        given-names: "Huanru Henry"
      - family-names: McAuley
        given-names: Julian
    collection-title: "Proceedings of the 19th International Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR)"
    year: 2018
    notes: >-
      SNES-MDB takes its format family and its composer-disjoint split
      convention from NES-MDB.
  - type: software
    title: snes_spc
    version: "0.9.0"
    authors:
      - name: "Shay Green (blargg)"
    license: LGPL-2.1
    url: "http://www.slack.net/~ant/libs/"
    notes: >-
      The SPC700/S-DSP emulator extraction depends on. Vendored under its own
      LGPL-2.1 license, with three marked instrumentation hooks added; see
      vendor/snes_spc/MODIFICATIONS.md.
